```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>",
                      fig.path = "figures/breeding-values-",
                      fig.width = 8, fig.height = 5, dpi = 80,
                      out.width = "100%")
```

An animal model answers "how much of the variation in this trait is additive"
and gives the covariance `G`. It does not answer "what is the additive value of
*this* opossum", because the breeding values are integrated out of the
likelihood: that is what makes the model scale to many individuals and many
traits. `breeding_values()` puts them back afterwards, from the `G`, `E` and
`beta` the fit already estimated. What comes out is what a breeding program
would act on: a predicted additive value per individual, with the reliability of
that prediction.

```{r packages, message = FALSE}
library(stanimal)
library(ggplot2)
```

## Fit

Three distances that span the skull and are not dominated by a single axis.

```{r fit, message = FALSE}
traits <- c("IS_NSL", "NA_BR", "ZI_ZYGO")
dat <- opossum[complete.cases(opossum[, c("id", "sex", "age", traits)]), ]
dat[traits] <- log(dat[traits])

design <- suppressWarnings(
  animal_design(cbind(IS_NSL, NA_BR, ZI_ZYGO) ~ sex + age, dat))
A <- opossum_grm[rownames(design$Y), rownames(design$Y)]
fit <- lmm_animal(design$Y, design$X, A, lkj_prior = 2,
                  iter = 800, warmup = 400, chains = 2,
                  refresh = 0, seed = 1)
```

## The breeding values

The prediction is the conditional mean of the additive values given the data,
evaluated at the posterior means of `G`, `E` and `beta`. That is empirical
Bayes: the standard errors are conditional on those values and do not carry the
uncertainty in the covariance parameters.

```{r ebv}
ebv <- breeding_values(fit)
ebv
```

The reliability is the share of a trait's additive variance the prediction pins
down. An animal with many measured relatives scores higher than a lone one.

```{r reliability}
round(apply(ebv$reliability, 2, stats::quantile, c(0.05, 0.5, 0.95)), 2)
```

The values are on the trait's scale, as deviations from the fixed effects, so
they can be compared with each animal's own deviation:

```{r rank-correlation}
residual <- design$Y - design$X %*% t(draws_mean(fit, "beta"))
round(diag(stats::cor(ebv$values, residual, method = "spearman")), 2)
```

## Ranking candidates

Ranking by breeding value and ranking by measurement do not give the same list.

```{r ranking}
first <- traits[1]
by_value <- rownames(ebv$values)[order(ebv$values[, first], decreasing = TRUE)]
by_phenotype <- rownames(residual)[order(residual[, first], decreasing = TRUE)]
data.frame(id = by_value[1:10],
           value = round(ebv$values[by_value[1:10], first], 3),
           se = round(ebv$se[by_value[1:10], first], 3),
           reliability = round(ebv$reliability[by_value[1:10], first], 3))
length(intersect(by_value[1:10], by_phenotype[1:10]))
```

Here `r length(intersect(by_value[1:10], by_phenotype[1:10]))` of the top ten by breeding value
are also in the top ten by their own measurement. With reliabilities around one
half, an animal's relatives and its other traits weigh as much in the
prediction as its own record.

## Shrinkage

Every prediction sits closer to the mean than the animal's own deviation, and
the points furthest inside the line are the ones with the lowest reliability.
Both axes are in phenotypic standard deviations, since each log distance has its
own scale.

```{r shrinkage, fig.height = 4}
sd_P <- sqrt(diag(draws_mean(fit, "P")))
plot_dat <- data.frame(
  trait = factor(rep(traits, each = nrow(residual)), levels = traits),
  measured = as.numeric(sweep(residual, 2, sd_P, "/")),
  predicted = as.numeric(sweep(ebv$values, 2, sd_P, "/")),
  reliability = as.numeric(ebv$reliability))

ggplot(plot_dat, aes(x = measured, y = predicted, colour = reliability)) +
  geom_abline(slope = 1, intercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey50") +
  geom_point(size = 1.2, alpha = 0.8) +
  scale_colour_viridis_c(option = "magma", direction = -1) +
  facet_wrap(~trait, scales = "free") +
  labs(x = "measured deviation from the fixed effects (phenotypic sds)",
       y = "predicted breeding value (phenotypic sds)",
       title = "Breeding values shrink the measurements toward the mean") +
  theme_minimal()
```
